1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,024
Dilnoza works in the bank's operations team, and on Monday she asks Timur for a small favour.

2
00:00:06,074 --> 00:00:11,592
Could the card zone get one more door, just an admin page, so she can look things up without a ticket?

3
00:00:11,642 --> 00:00:17,088
Timur says no, kindly, and his reason is the whole design of the card zone.

4
00:00:17,138 --> 00:00:24,264
In Lesson 11, buyers' browsers reached the card frame, and in Lesson 7, ecom's calls reached the card zone.

5
00:00:24,314 --> 00:00:28,704
This lesson shows the two doors they came through, and why nothing else listens.

6
00:00:28,754 --> 00:00:31,080
The card zone has two kinds of visitor.

7
00:00:31,130 --> 00:00:34,920
Buyers' browsers come from the internet, to type a card into the frame.

8
00:00:34,970 --> 00:00:40,728
Services from other products, like ecom, come from their own servers, to ask for a charge.

9
00:00:40,778 --> 00:00:42,720
One big entrance could serve both.

10
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:47,280
But it would open every visitor's traffic, and see it all in clear text.

11
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:50,712
That entrance would become the most valuable box in the bank.

12
00:00:50,762 --> 00:00:55,776
So there are two narrow doors: a counter for the public, and a mail slot for partners.

13
00:00:55,826 --> 00:00:58,128
The counter is the payers' door.

14
00:00:58,178 --> 00:01:01,416
A buyer's browser connects to it over an encrypted line.

15
00:01:01,466 --> 00:01:06,024
The counter opens that line with a certificate that every browser trusts.

16
00:01:06,074 --> 00:01:12,144
Then it passes on only the card frame's few requests: show the frame, send a card, send a code.

17
00:01:12,194 --> 00:01:15,264
Every other request gets the answer "not found".

18
00:01:15,314 --> 00:01:20,472
It also limits how fast one buyer can submit cards, at the bank's chosen rate.

19
00:01:20,522 --> 00:01:23,184
It never limits simply loading the page.

20
00:01:23,234 --> 00:01:25,536
The mail slot is the callers' door.

21
00:01:25,586 --> 00:01:27,984
A caller's connection arrives sealed.

22
00:01:28,034 --> 00:01:31,536
On the outside of the envelope is the name of the service it wants.

23
00:01:31,586 --> 00:01:33,552
The door reads only that name.

24
00:01:33,602 --> 00:01:39,240
It never opens the envelope, and passes it on, still sealed, to the service behind it.

25
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:42,888
Passing a connection along unopened is called pass-through.

26
00:01:42,938 --> 00:01:47,736
Only a few names pass: the card vault, the adapters, and the login server.

27
00:01:47,786 --> 00:01:51,336
Any other name, or no name at all, is refused.

28
00:01:51,386 --> 00:01:57,312
The service behind the door then checks the caller itself, with the caller's certificate and its token.

29
00:01:57,362 --> 00:02:00,144
So the door never needs a key to anyone's traffic.

30
00:02:00,194 --> 00:02:03,384
Timur shows Dilnoza what each visitor meets.

31
00:02:03,434 --> 00:02:06,000
A buyer opens Malika's payment page.

32
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:10,536
The browser shows its padlock, because the counter's certificate is trusted.

33
00:02:10,586 --> 00:02:13,296
The frame loads, and the buyer types a card.

34
00:02:13,346 --> 00:02:18,744
Then Timur tries Dilnoza's idea: an admin page address on the same door.

35
00:02:18,794 --> 00:02:21,072
The answer is plain: not found.

36
00:02:21,122 --> 00:02:23,400
Nothing behind the door is even named.

37
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:28,320
Next, he presses Send code again and again, very fast, the way a script would.

38
00:02:28,370 --> 00:02:30,192
The counter steps in.

39
00:02:30,242 --> 00:02:34,464
The frame says: too many tries too fast, try again in a minute.

40
00:02:34,514 --> 00:02:37,200
After the wait, the session still works.

41
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:42,480
On the callers' side, a service whose name is not on the list knocks on the mail slot.

42
00:02:42,530 --> 00:02:44,784
The connection simply closes.

43
00:02:44,834 --> 00:02:46,776
It never reaches the card vault.

44
00:02:46,826 --> 00:02:53,448
A listed name without a valid certificate gets past the slot, and is then refused by the service itself.

45
00:02:53,498 --> 00:02:58,536
And if any part stops answering, monitoring raises an alert for Timur's team.

46
00:02:58,586 --> 00:03:05,136
Here is my position: every convenient door into a card zone becomes the door an attacker uses.

47
00:03:05,186 --> 00:03:09,288
With two narrow doors, the counter sees only the frame's traffic.

48
00:03:09,338 --> 00:03:11,592
The mail slot holds nothing it can read.

49
00:03:11,642 --> 00:03:15,360
The database, the keys and the logs have no door at all.

50
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:20,016
And the bank's firewalls stay simple: two openings, each with one job.

51
00:03:20,066 --> 00:03:24,936
Dilnoza still gets her answers, from the admin portal, outside the card zone.

52
00:03:24,986 --> 00:03:27,576
Next: how a caller gets its certificate.
